# Inverse State Consistency Decision Record

The inverse state consistency that appeared in the 2025.3 compatibility train was initially handled as a Quality and Regression issue in May. At the time, the full invocation was unavailable, so the only available option was to leave the existing investigation entry point at `src/engine/engine_forward.c`.

There were three directions at the time: inspect state and results along the forward adjacency; trace state transfer first; or find the caller first. The first two terms appeared more often in existing discussions, while the third direction had no entry point. The decision was to start with the first direction and treat "the same state is comparable at the forward adjacency" as a sufficient approximation.

The reason for this decision is simple: the file path already narrows the investigation to a specific location, while the caller, the result seen by the user, and the state-transfer process are all undocumented. The train will not stop because of these gaps; the judgment will be revised if a counterexample appears later.

The open questions are:

- What is the actual invocation form of inverse;
- Who holds the state before and after the invocation;
- Is the result an acceleration, a force, or some other output;
- Is `engine_forward.c` merely an adjacency entry point.

The May position was to initially treat forward-side consistency as an acceptable stand-in for inverse state consistency.
